<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Developer scenario embark mode. Loads only inside a throwaway scenario profile, because the
  Harness directory is absent from the shipped manifest Directories and excluded from
  Tools/stage.sh; an ordinary build never sees this file and the mode cannot be selected.

  The mode sets the scenario request before world build. Qud's gamemode descriptor applies
  stringgamestate values during bootGame, which is ahead of the player-mutator step where
  KingdomScenarioNewGameGate proves the request and writes the stamp.

  The same request is ALSO read in-chargen, straight off the parsed mode descriptor, by
  KingdomScenarioFastEmbarkModule below: that is how the frozen seed reaches EmbarkInfo.GameSeed
  without anyone typing it. Tools/prepare-scenario.sh rewrites the Value below and then seals it,
  and Tools/run-scenario.ps1 re-proves the overlay still carries exactly the sealed request, so the
  seed the mode applies is the seed the gate later demands the engine generated under.

  Phase 1 authors one scenario, so one mode carries it. A later phase replaces this with a
  starting-location Set filter once the roster holds more than one row.
-->
<embarkmodules>
  <module Class="XRL.CharacterBuilds.Qud.QudGamemodeModule">
    <modes>
      <mode ID="TAFScenario" Title="[Dev] TAF scenario">
        <icon Tile="Items/sw_book_1.bmp" Foreground="y" Detail="W"></icon>
        <description>
          Developer scenario harness. Builds a pinned-seed state for review. Never ships: this mode exists only inside an isolated scenario profile, and any verdict taken here is ineligible until its authority class has a green ordinary-play differential anchor.
        </description>
        <stringgamestate Name="GameMode" Value="Kingdom"></stringgamestate>
        <boolgamestate Name="r_TAF_KingdomMode" Value="true"></boolgamestate>
        <stringgamestate Name="r_TAF_ScenarioRequest_v1" Value="arch-gallery-slice;facing=north"></stringgamestate>
        <gamesystem Class="ThousandAndFirst.KingdomSuccession" />
        <!--
          Unattended scenario execution. bootGame hands every declared class to XRLGame.AddSystem,
          which resolves it through ModManager.CreateInstance, so a mod type is reached by name -
          the same route the succession system above already takes. The runner is an IPlayerSystem
          that primes on ZoneActivatedEvent - raising the engine's own Popup.Suppress so the boot
          and arrival popups auto-acknowledge instead of waiting on a keypress - and then runs the
          script ONCE, on the first player action opportunity in the built world. It is inert unless
          the profile's sealed Local/scenario-script.txt exists, suppresses nothing without one, and
          never quits the game.
        -->
        <gamesystem Class="ThousandAndFirst.KingdomScenarioAutoRunner" />
      </mode>
    </modes>
  </module>

  <!--
    Dev start location. EmbarkBuilderConfiguration.Init keys its module dictionary by class name, so
    this block MERGES into the base game's own QudChooseStartingLocationModule instance rather than
    replacing it, and the location handler appends by ID - every stock location keeps working.

    WHY NOT JOPPA. The scenario script's first verb prepares ground, and Joppa's zone is a built
    village: the gallery's clearance law lawfully refuses most of it, so a run would spend its one
    profile discovering that.

    WHY 6.17, AND WHY NOT 11.21. This default was JoppaWorld.11.21, described here as open
    wilderness one parasang north of Joppa. The base game's own world map says otherwise:
    Base/QudWorldMap.rpm paints parasang 11,21 as TerrainJoppaRedrockChannel, a named channel cell,
    not generic wilderness. JoppaWorld.6.17 is TerrainSaltdunes - the canonical salt-dune world
    terrain, DefaultWall Halite, no village or ruin painted on it - and every one of its eight
    neighbours is salt dunes too, so the 3x3 parasang region a future arcology persona needs is
    homogeneous. Nothing in JoppaWorldBuilder hardcodes a zone anywhere near it (its nearest
    hardcoded coordinates are 11.22 and 22.14). It is in bounds with margin: the engine's biome
    arrays are exactly 80x25 parasangs and the whole 3x3 spans x=5..7, y=16..18. Cell 40,12 is the
    middle of an 80x25 zone.

    Procedural content (lairs, ruins, wandering creatures) is seed-dependent and no static map can
    rule it out, so if the zone still refuses, flatten says so by name in the journal and the
    operator reruns with `ground` ahead of it, or re-prepares with TAF_SCENARIO_START set to another
    parasang. Tools/prepare-scenario.sh rewrites the Location below inside the throwaway profile.

    NO Set ATTRIBUTE, on purpose. QudChooseStartingLocationModuleWindow filters by `Set ==
    currentSet`, and QudGamemodeModule answers that query with null for every mode but Tutorial, so
    a Set value here would hide this entry from its own mode's carousel. Containment is not the
    filter, it is this file: Harness/ is absent from the shipped manifest and excluded from
    Tools/stage.sh, so an ordinary build never loads a line of it. ExcludeFromDaily keeps the dev
    tile out of Daily's random pick regardless.

    The grid must carry ALL FIFTEEN positions: StartingLocationControl.UpdateTiles indexes its
    originals dictionary by the prefab's own tile keys and throws on a missing one, and
    GetBreadcrumb reads "21" directly.
  -->
  <module Class="XRL.CharacterBuilds.Qud.QudChooseStartingLocationModule">
    <locations>
      <location ID="TAFTestGround" Name="{{W|[Dev] TAF}}&#10;test ground" Location="GlobalLocation:JoppaWorld.6.17.1.1.10@40,12" ExcludeFromDaily="Yes">
        <description>
          Developer scenario harness. Open salt dunes in the ivory sea west of Joppa, chosen so the scenario script's ground preparation has somewhere to work and so a 3x3-parasang region around it is one uniform terrain. Never ships.
        </description>

        <grid Position="00" Tile="Terrain/sw_plains.bmp" Foreground="Y" Detail="Y" Background="k"></grid>
        <grid Position="10" Tile="Terrain/sw_desert.bmp" Foreground="Y" Detail="Y" Background="k"></grid>
        <grid Position="20" Tile="Terrain/sw_plains.bmp" Foreground="Y" Detail="Y" Background="k"></grid>
        <grid Position="30" Tile="Terrain/sw_plains.bmp" Foreground="Y" Detail="Y" Background="k"></grid>
        <grid Position="40" Tile="Terrain/sw_plains.bmp" Foreground="Y" Detail="Y" Background="k"></grid>

        <grid Position="01" Tile="Terrain/sw_desert.bmp" Foreground="Y" Detail="Y" Background="k"></grid>
        <grid Position="11" Tile="Terrain/sw_plains.bmp" Foreground="Y" Detail="Y" Background="k"></grid>
        <grid Position="21" Tile="Items/sw_book_1.bmp" Foreground="y" Detail="W" Background="k"></grid>
        <grid Position="31" Tile="Terrain/sw_plains.bmp" Foreground="Y" Detail="Y" Background="k"></grid>
        <grid Position="41" Tile="Terrain/sw_desert.bmp" Foreground="Y" Detail="Y" Background="k"></grid>

        <grid Position="02" Tile="Terrain/sw_plains.bmp" Foreground="Y" Detail="Y" Background="k"></grid>
        <grid Position="12" Tile="Terrain/sw_plains.bmp" Foreground="Y" Detail="Y" Background="k"></grid>
        <grid Position="22" Tile="Terrain/sw_plains.bmp" Foreground="Y" Detail="Y" Background="k"></grid>
        <grid Position="32" Tile="Terrain/sw_desert.bmp" Foreground="Y" Detail="Y" Background="k"></grid>
        <grid Position="42" Tile="Terrain/sw_desert.bmp" Foreground="Y" Detail="Y" Background="k"></grid>
      </location>
    </locations>
  </module>

  <!--
    Fast embark. Carries no windows and no data of its own: it exists to pre-fill the flow the
    moment the mode above is selected, and is inert for every other mode. Registered by name -
    EmbarkBuilderConfiguration resolves module classes through ModManager.ResolveType, which
    searches mod assemblies before the game assembly. Element form mirrors the base game's own
    windowless module rows.
  -->
  <module Class="ThousandAndFirst.Harness.KingdomScenarioFastEmbarkModule">

  </module>

  <!--
    Born-clean test ground. Also windowless, and also inert outside the scenario mode. It binds a
    post-builder to whatever starting zone the flow actually settled on, on the BootStartingLocation
    boot event - which QudGameBootModule fires long before GlobalLocation.ResolveCell generates that
    zone - so the persona starts on flat bare passable ground instead of on whatever worldgen
    painted. Worldgen places villages, ruins, and lairs by seed, so no hardcoded start parasang can
    be trusted: JoppaWorld.11.21 was live-confirmed to hold a village. Declaring the ground is the
    durable answer; the parasang below is now only a default.

    Inert without a sealed script, because an attended profile is one an operator is driving by hand
    and silently rewriting the world under them is exactly the surprise this harness must not spring.
  -->
  <module Class="ThousandAndFirst.Harness.KingdomScenarioTestGroundModule">

  </module>
</embarkmodules>
